    "tradeoff": {
      "title": "Cut, and called wrong",
      "intro": "A resume only adds. These are the subtractions — kept here because they are the part that took judgement.",
      "groups": [
        {
          "label": "Called wrong",
          "items": [
            {
              "h3": "HEYTEA — got the outward product right, then over-invested inward",
              "p": "HEYTEA GO was the right call: a user-facing tool that, once live, overturned internal processes and exposed what was wrong with them. The mistake came after. Internal requests kept arriving and we poured effort into internal process — but outward work is growth and inward work is the existing stock, and that stock was already being rewritten by the very change the outward product was driving. A good deal of the internal work we carefully optimised stopped being work at all."
            },
            {
              "h3": "Vika — over-tuned the experience, under-served the enterprise",
              "p": "We spent too much on interaction polish and tilted too far toward the individual user, and so missed what large enterprises actually needed. In data-heavy work, experience is not what they are buying. The AI era makes that misjudgement worse rather than milder: what consumes software now is an agent, not a person. It does not want an experience — it wants interfaces, constraints and results it can verify. The part I invested least in turned out to be the part that mattered."
            },
            {
              "h3": "bika.ai — called too early",
              "p": "I bet that Level-5 AGI would make the <em>organizer</em> the central product form, and invested ahead of it. The tool itself is genuinely capable. What I misjudged was interaction: it stayed interface-driven while coding agents moved to a fluid, conversational loop. Adoption was decided by that interaction gap, not by capability."
            }
          ],
          "thread": "What the three have in common: what I got wrong was never capability. It was who the work is for — outward or inward, a person or an agent, an individual user or an enterprise."
        },
        {
          "label": "Cut",
          "items": [
            {
              "h3": "A 40% reduction — first here, then with clients",
              "p": "I ran the agent transition inside my own company before selling it, and now help clients reach the same 40% reduction in work that used to need people. The trade-off is real: once agents do the work, throughput stops being capped by headcount and starts being capped by review capacity."
            },
            {
              "h3": "Pushed back on the “one-person company” story",
              "p": "The popular version says agents replace the organization. Having actually run it, I have said publicly that they do not. The binding constraint simply moves to how much you can review — and review does not parallelise the way generation does."
            }
          ],
          "thread": null
        }
      ]
    },
